  {
   "id": "k3-7",
   "name": "A mole of atoms and the Avogadro constant"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "One mole of any substance contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles (atoms, molecules or ions). This number is the Avogadro constant, L or Nₐ.\n• Number of particles = moles × 6.02 × 10²³.\n• Moles = number of particles ÷ 6.02 × 10²³.\n• 12 g of carbon (1 mol) and 24 g of magnesium (1 mol) contain the same number of atoms: 6.02 × 10²³.\nCombine with mass: atoms = (mass ÷ molar mass) × 6.02 × 10²³.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "learn": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "How many in a mole?",
    "text": "A dozen means {12}; a ream of paper means {500} sheets. A mole means {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} particles.\nThis huge number is the Avogadro constant (L). It was chosen so that {12} g of carbon-{12} contains exactly one mole of atoms.\n{6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} is {602000} million million million: if you counted one atom every second, it would take far longer than the age of the universe."
   },
   {
    "heading": "Particles and moles",
    "text": "• particles = moles × {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)}\n• moles = particles ÷ ({6.02} × {10}{sup(23)})\n{2} mol of copper contains {2} × {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} = {12.04} × {10}{sup(23)} = {1.204} × {10}{sup(24)} atoms.\n{3.01} × {10}{sup(23)} atoms is half a mole."
   },
   {
    "heading": "From grams to atoms",
    "text": "Go through moles:\n• grams → moles: divide by the molar mass;\n• moles → atoms: multiply by {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)}.\n{46} g of sodium (Na = {23}) is {2} mol, which is {12.04} × {10}{sup(23)} atoms. Equal numbers of moles always contain equal numbers of particles."
   }
  ],
  "key_words": [
   {
    "word": "Avogadro constant",
    "meaning": "{6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} particles per mole"
   },
   {
    "word": "particle",
    "meaning": "an atom, molecule or ion"
   },
   {
    "word": "standard form",
    "meaning": "a × {10}ⁿ with {1} ≤ a < {10}"
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   "{0.5} mol = {3.01} × {10}{sup(23)} atoms",
   "{3} mol = {1.806} × {10}{sup(24)} atoms",
   "{64} g of copper (Cu = {64}) = {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} atoms"
  ],
  "mistake": {
   "wrong": "{2} mol = {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} atoms",
   "right": "{2} mol = {2} × {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} = {1.204} × {10}{sup(24)} atoms",
   "why": "Each mole contains {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} particles; multiply by the number of moles."
  }
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": 2
  },
  "problem": "How many atoms are in {n} mol of copper? Give the answer in standard form.",
  "steps": [
   "Atoms = moles × {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)}.",
   "= {n} × {6.02} × {10}{sup(23)} = {n * 6.02} × {10}{sup(23)}.",
   "In standard form: {n * 0.602} × {10}{sup(24)}."
  ],
  "answer": "{n * 0.602} × {10}{sup(24)} atoms",
  "checks": [
   "n * 0.602 == 1.204"
  ]
 },
